    <statement><p>Consider the sequence <m>(a_n)_{n \ge 0}</m> which starts <m>2, 14, 26, 38, \ldots\text{.}</m></p>
<p><ol label="a.">
<li>
<p>What is the next term in the sequence?</p>
<p><fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Find a formula for the <m>n</m>th term of this sequence.</p>
<p><m>a_n =</m> <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Find the sum of the first 100 terms of the sequence: <m>\sum_{k=0}^{99}a_k\text{.}</m></p>
<p><fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>50</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>2+12n</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>59600</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p><ol label="a.">
<li>
<p><m>50\text{,}</m> which is <m>38+12\text{.}</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p>The sequence is arithmetic, with <m>a_0 = 2</m> and constant difference 12, so <m>a_n = 2 + 12n\text{.}</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>59600\text{.}</m>  We want <m>2 + 26 + \cdots + 1190\text{.}</m>  Reverse and add to get 100 sums of 1192, a total of 119200, which is twice the sum we are looking for.</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</solution>
